{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789464121,"nonce":"vp8n7uGR5IYMgNDYd7p2IEs6Nys=","title":"LLVM promotion recompiles the promoted function's whole callee closure","message":"`LLVMJitBackend::compile_function` builds a temporary module of the promoted function plus everything `reachable_from_roots` finds from it and compiles all of it, so LLVM can inline through the callees; only the root's entry is published, the callee copies are private to that module. On a ZynML kernel the closure is a handful of functions (binary_trees: 4). On a Python program `main` reaches the library, about 170 functions, so one promotion would recompile the reachable library on a background thread holding the LLVM lock. Nothing is compiled ahead of time; `set_module_context` only hands the HIR over.\n\nPromotion should compile the one function, with each callee declared extern and bound to its current entry, read through the reload cell so a callee promoted later is reached too.\n\nWhat stands in the way is the aggregate convention. Cranelift carries a struct parameter as a pointer to its storage and returns a struct through a caller-provided destination (`returns_through_destination`); the LLVM tier lowers `HirType::Struct` as an LLVM struct by value. Inside one closure this never shows. LLVM code calling Cranelift code would cross it at every `List`-taking library function, and the same mismatch is latent today for any promoted root whose own signature carries an aggregate, since its entry is published into a bead Cranelift callers use. So the order is: one aggregate convention for both tiers (the pointer-and-destination one Cranelift uses is the simpler to adopt in the LLVM declaration path), then per-function promotion with externally bound callees, then measure whether LLVM's lost cross-function inlining costs anything the HIR inliner has not already recovered.","files":null}]}